{"page": 39, "text": "(50,095)\n\u2014\n\u2014\n(50,095)\nSelling, general and administrative expense\n(52,104)\n(57,400)\n(49,121)\n(46,404)\n(70,412)\n\u2014\n(869)\n(93,181)\n(369,491)\nOther segment items(2)\n(3,389)\n(9,418)\n(470)\n(2,180)\n(2,249)\n\u2014\n605\n(3,930)\n(21,031)\nIncome (loss) before tax expense\n$\n67,590\n$\n53,363\n$\n23,574\n$\n38,559\n$\n48,947\n$\n11,571\n$\n(264)\n$\n(95,901)\n$\n147,439\n(1)Beginning in the fourth quarter of 2025, inventory impairment was reclassified to be included in cost of home sales revenues in our consolidated statements of operations rather than presented as a separate line item and prior year period amounts have been reclassified to conform to this presentation.\n(2)Includes cost of land sales and other revenues, and other income (expense).\nWest\nDuring the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, our West segment generated loss before income tax expense of $13.5 million and income before income tax expense of $6.6 million, respectively, representing decreases of 163.4% and 90.2%, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods. These decreases were primarily driven by decreases in homebuilding gross margin percentage and revenue. Homebuilding gross margin percentage decreased from the respective prior year periods, due to impairment charges of $19.1 million during the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, as well as increased use of incentives as compared to the respective prior year periods. Revenue decreased $65.0 million and $107.8 million, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods, primarily driven by decreases of 24.7% and 12.9%, respectively, in the number of homes delivered and decreases of 6.8% and 5.8%, respectively, in the average sales price per home. The decreases in the number of homes delivered were primarily attributable to slower absorption rates and the decreases in average sales price per home resulted from increased use of incentives.\nMountain\n"}
{"page": 40, "text": "During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, our Mountain segment generated income before income tax expense of $6.7 million and $28.9 million, respectively, representing decreases of 54.6% and 45.8%, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods. These decreases were primarily driven by decreases in revenue and homebuilding gross margin percentage. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, revenue decreased $27.7 million and $100.7 million, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods, primarily driven by decreases of 3.7% and 6.5%, respectively, in the number of homes delivered and decreases of 7.7% and 9.1%, respectively, in the average sales price per home. The decreases in the number of homes delivered were primarily attributable to slower absorption rates and the decreases in average sales price per home resulted from increased use of incentives. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, homebuilding gross margin percentage decreased from the respective prior year periods, due primarily to increased use of incentives as compared to the respective prior year periods.\nTexas\nDuring the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, our Texas segment generated income before income tax expense of $6.9 million and $20.2 million, respectively, representing an increase of 35.8% and a decrease of 14.2%, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods. The three-month increase was primarily driven by increases in homebuilding gross margin percentage and revenue, while the nine-month decrease was primarily driven by an increase in selling, general and administrative expense and a decrease in revenue. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, revenue increased $19.4 million and decreased $2.1 million, respectively. The three-month increase was primarily driven by a 17.4% increase in the number of homes delivered, partially offset by a 2.6% decrease in the average sales price per home, while the nine-month decrease was primarily driven by a 2.7% decrease in the average sales price per home, partially offset by a 1.4% increase in the number of homes delivered. The increases in homes delivered were primarily attributable to an increase in the average number of selling communities, while the decreases in average sales price per home were primarily driven by increased use of incentives. Homebuilding gross margin percentage for each of the three and nine month ended September 30, 2026 increased from the respective prior year periods, due primarily to lower direct construction costs and lower\n26\nTable of Contents\ninventory impairment charges of $0.2 million during the current year periods compared to $0.5 million in the prior year periods, partially offset by increased use of incentives.\nSoutheast\n"}
{"page": 41, "text": "During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, our Southeast segment generated income before income tax expense of $9.0 million and $39.9 million, respectively, representing a decrease of 31.5% and an increase of 3.4%, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods. The decrease in income before income tax expense for the three-month comparison was primarily driven by a decrease in revenue, partially offset by an increase in homebuilding gross margin percentage. The increase in income before income tax expense for the nine-month comparison was primarily driven by a land sale transaction in Georgia during the first quarter of 2026, partially offset by a decrease in home sales revenue. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, revenue decreased $54.3 million and $65.8 million, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods. The decrease in revenue for the three-month comparison was primarily driven by a 26.7% decrease in the number of homes delivered and a 5.8% decrease in the average sales price per home. The decrease in revenue for the nine-month comparison was primarily driven by a 12.4% decrease in the number of homes delivered and a 9.1% decrease in the average sales price per home, partially offset by the first quarter land sale transaction of $32.5 million included in land sales and other revenue. The decreases in the number of homes delivered were primarily attributable to a decrease in the average number of selling communities, while the decreases in average sales price per home resulted primarily from increased use of incentives. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, homebuilding gross margin percentage increased from the respective prior year periods, due primarily to inventory impairment charges of $2.4 million and $6.2 million recorded in the prior year periods, respectively, that did not recur in the current year periods, and was partially offset by the impact of increased use of incentives as compared to the respective prior year periods.\nCentury Complete\n"}
{"page": 42, "text": "During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, our Century Complete segment generated income before income tax expense of $20.9 million and $55.3 million, respectively, representing increases of 3.8% and 12.9%, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods. The increase in income before income tax expense for the three-month comparison was primarily driven by an increase in revenue, partially offset by a decrease in homebuilding gross margin percentage. The increase in income before income tax expense for the nine-month comparison was primarily driven by an increase in homebuilding gross margin percentage, partially offset by a decrease in revenue. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, revenue increased $10.0 million and decreased $33.9 million, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods. The increase in revenue for the three-month comparison was primarily driven by an 11.4% increase in the number of homes delivered, partially offset by a 6.3% decrease in the average sales price per home. The decrease in revenue for the nine-month comparison was primarily driven by a 2.6% decrease in the number of homes delivered and a 2.5% decrease in the average sales price per home. The increase in the number of homes delivered for the three-month comparison was primarily driven by a higher average number of selling communities, while the decrease in the number of homes delivered for the nine-month comparison was primarily driven by a lower average number of selling communities and slower absorption rates. The decreases in average sales price per home resulted primarily from increased use of incentives. For the three-month comparison, homebuilding gross margin percentage decreased, primarily driven by increased use of incentives. For the nine-month comparison, homebuilding gross margin percentage increased, driven primarily by lower direct construction costs and lower inventory impairment charges of $0.3 million compared to $4.2 million in the prior year period, partially offset by increased use of incentives as compared to the prior year period.\nFinancial Services\n"}
{"page": 43, "text": "Our Financial Services segment originates mortgages for primarily our homebuyers, and as such, the volume of loans originated typically correlates to our number of homes delivered. Fluctuations in financial services income before income tax may occur because some components of revenue fluctuate differently than loan volumes, and some expenses are not directly related to mortgage loan volume or to changes in the amount of revenue earned. For the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, our Financial Services segment generated income before income tax expense of $6.2 million and $23.7 million, respectively, as compared to $3.0 million and $11.6 million, respectively, in the respective prior year periods. For the three-month comparison, the increase was primarily attributable to higher loan margins, driven by favorable fair value adjustments on our hedging activity, and higher servicing fee income, partially offset by lower origination volume. For the nine-month comparison, the increase was primarily attributable to higher loans margins, driven by favorable fair value adjustments on our hedging activity, and favorable fair value adjustments on our mortgage servicing rights portfolio, partially offset by the prior year benefit from the sale of a portion of our mortgage servicing rights portfolio and lower origination volume. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, the number of mortgages originated decreased 1.6% and 2.5%, respectively, in each case, as compared to the respective prior year period. During the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, the number of loans sold to third parties increased 5.9% and 0.2%, respectively, as compared to the respective prior year periods.\n27\nTable of Contents\nThe following table presents selected operational data for our Financial Services segment in relation to our loan origination activities (dollars in thousands):\nThree Months Ended September 30,\nNine Months Ended September 30,\n2026\n2025\n2026\n2025\nTotal originations:\nNumber of loans\n1,581\n1,607\n4,676\n4,796\nPrincipal\n$\n541,461\n$\n578,011\n$\n1,610,188\n$\n1,700,470\nCapture rate of Century homebuyers\n84\n%\n84\n%\n82\n%\n84\n%\nCentury Communities\n87\n%\n89\n%\n86\n%\n86\n%\nCentury Complete\n78\n%\n74\n%\n76\n%\n78\n%\nAverage FICO score\n719\n727\n720\n726\nCentury Communities\n722\n730\n722\n731\nCentury Complete\n714\n722\n713\n716\nLoans sold to third parties:\nNumber of loans sold\n1,715\n1,619\n4,966\n4,957\nPrincipal\n$\n583,726\n$\n580,971\n$\n1,718,591\n$\n1,755,136\nCentury Living\n"}
{"page": 44, "text": "Our Century Living operations are engaged in the development, construction, management, and sales of multi-family rental properties, currently all located in Colorado. For the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, our Century Living segment generated losses before income tax expense of $155 thousand and $203 thousand, respectively, as compared to losses before income tax expense of $298 thousand and $264 thousand, respectively, in the respective prior year periods. As of September 30, 2026, the Company had three multi-family rental properties in Colorado, of which two were available for leasing. As of September 30, 2026, these three projects represented 868 total multi-family units, including 251 under active construction and 617 completed units, and 505 units were leased. As of September 30, 2025, we had three multi-family rental properties, two of which were completed and one of which was under active construction. We subsequently sold one multi-family rental property during the fourth quarter of 2025.\nCorporate\nDuring the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, our Corporate segment generated losses of $20.3 million and $76.2 million, respectively, as compared to losses of $29.3 million and $95.9 million, respectively, during the respective prior year periods. The decreases were primarily related to lower general and administrative costs, including lower charitable contributions and decreased compensation related to adjustments in stock-based compensation expense to reflect a decreased estimate in the number of shares which will ultimately vest and be issued upon settlement of certain performance share unit awards.\nHomebuilding Gross Margin\nHomebuilding gross margin represents home sales revenues less cost of home sales revenues. Our homebuilding gross margin percentage, which represents homebuilding gross margin divided by home sales revenues, decreased to 15.0% for the three months ended September 30, 2026 from 17.9% for the prior year period and decreased to 17.0% for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 from 18.4% for the prior year period. The decrease for the three-month comparison was primarily driven by inventory impairment charges of $19.6 million, compared to $3.2 million in the prior year period, as well as increased use of incentives. The decrease for the nine-month comparison was primarily driven by increased use of incentives, as well as higher inventory impairment charges of $19.6 million, compared to $11.0 million in the prior year period.\n28\nTable of Contents\n"}
