{"page": 46, "text": "For the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, our adjusted homebuilding gross margin percentage, excluding inventory impairment, interest in cost of home sales revenues, and purchase price accounting for acquired work in process inventory, was 19.3% and 19.7%, respectively, as compared to 20.1% and 20.6%, respectively, for the same respective periods in 2025. We believe the above non-GAAP adjusted homebuilding gross margin information is meaningful as it isolates the impact that inventory impairment, indebtedness, and acquisitions (in each case as applicable) during any period, have on our homebuilding gross margin and allows for comparability of our homebuilding gross margins to prior year periods and to homebuilding gross margins of our competitors.\n29\nTable of Contents\nSelling, General and Administrative Expense\n(dollars in thousands)\nThree Months Ended September 30,\nChange\n2026\n2025\nAmount\n%\nSelling, general and administrative expense\n$\n118,837\n$\n119,895\n$\n(1,058)\n(0.9)\n%\nAs a percentage of home sales revenue\n14.1\n%\n12.6\n%\nNine Months Ended September 30,\nChange\n2026\n2025\nAmount\n%\nSelling, general and administrative expense\n$\n362,335\n$\n369,491\n$\n(7,156)\n(1.9)\n%\nAs a percentage of home sales revenue\n14.6\n%\n13.1\n%\nOur selling, general and administrative expense decreased $1.1 million and $7.2 million, respectively, for the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026 as compared to the same respective periods in 2025, primarily driven by decreased commissions expense, decreased charitable contributions and decreased compensation related to adjustments in stock-based compensation expense to reflect a decreased estimate in the number of shares which will ultimately vest and be issued upon settlement of certain performance share unit awards. As a percentage of home sales revenue, our selling, general and administrative expense increased 150 basis points during each of the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026, respectively, driven primarily by decreased revenue on a partially fixed cost base.\nIncome Tax Expense\nAt the end of each interim period, we are required to estimate our annual effective tax rate for the fiscal year and to use that rate to provide for income taxes for the current year-to-date reporting period. Our 2026 estimated annual effective tax rate, before discrete items, of 26.0%, is driven by our blended federal and state statutory rate of 24.6%, and certain permanent differences between GAAP and tax, including disallowed deductions for executive compensation, partially offset by estimated federal energy home credits for current year home deliveries, which combined resulted in a net increase in our effective tax rate of 1.4%.\nFor the nine months ended September 30, 2026, our estimated annual rate of 26.0% was impacted by discrete items which decreased our rate by 0.5%, primarily reflecting favorable adjustments to prior-year income tax estimates upon completion of our federal income tax return.\n"}
